div.p:
5. We learn hence, thirdly, that none but a Christian is happy; none but a real, inward Christian. A glutton, a drunkard, a gamester may be ‘merry’; but he cannot be happy. The beau, the belle, may eat and drink, and rise up to play;
See Exod. 32:6. but still they feel they are not happy. Men or women may adorn their own dear persons with all the colours of the rainbow. They may dance and sing, and hurry to and fro, and flutter hither and thither. They may roll up and down in their splendid carriages and talk insipidly to each other. They may hasten from one diversion to another; but happiness is not there. They are still ‘walking in a vain shadow, and disquieting themselves in vain’.
Cf. Ps. 39:7 (BCP). One of their own poets has truly pronounced concerning the whole life of these sons of pleasure:
